                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/13914: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bucknell</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-09-19T20:00:00+01:00">September 19, 1938—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 19—3:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>212. My telegram 211, September 19, 7 p.m. I learn that at the private meeting of
                    the Council this afternoon no opposition was made to the Chinese request for the
                    application of article 17. Poland was not represented on the Council on the
                    ground it is understood that Poland’s term has expired. It is believed here that
                    the real explanation of Poland’s taking this position is a desire to avoid being
                    represented on the Council in the event of a Czech appeal or protest to the
                    League.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucknell</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
